## Overview

Bite Buddy is a mealtime companion for young children. It's a plate with a curved screen around its rim that tracks how much of the meal has been eaten by weight and plays a simple game as the child eats, paired with an app for parents. It was our semester thesis project, guided by Dr. Pankaj Upadhyay, and ran for eight weeks.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="8 weeks" label="Project duration" /%}
{% stat value="3" label="Detachable plates in the final concept" /%}
{% stat value="6" label="Students in the brainstorming session" /%}
{% /stats %}

## The problem

Screens have become a common distraction for children at mealtimes. That can lead to more snacking later, makes it harder for children to notice whether they're still hungry or already full, and over time can affect their health and wellbeing. One study we came across, a survey in Lithuania, found that more than half of the children were exposed to screens during meals, 22% of them daily or at every meal.

We framed the problem like this: distractions from screen media at mealtimes can lead to mindless eating and overconsumption, so there's a need for something that encourages mindful eating and reduces the effect of screen media on children's health. We focused on children aged 3 to 5 and on the parents or caregivers who worry about this.

## My role

I worked on this with Harinandh V R. Between the two of us we covered the research, ideation, concept sketching, the physical product and its interactions, the device screens, and the companion app's visual language and UI.

## Process

We moved through five stages: understand, define, ideate, conceptualise and design.

### Understanding what already exists

We looked at existing products meant to make meals more fun for kids. Most were either limited to certain kinds of food or were simply a plate in an unusual shape, like a race track, a face or a taco truck. Methods commonly recommended to parents include removing screens from the dining area, setting a meal structure, making food appealing and giving children utensils sized for them.

![Existing mealtime products for children](/work/bite-buddy/market-research.webp "Existing products: mostly shaped plates or single-food tools")

### Brainstorming

To get a wide spread of ideas, we ran a brainstorming session with a group of six students. The goal was to generate as many ideas as possible on the topic. We then looked for ideas that kept coming up.

![Sticky notes from the brainstorming session](/work/bite-buddy/brainstorming.webp "Brainstorming with six students")

Four patterns became our selection criteria. The product should be reward-based, playful and interactive, use storytelling to make the game more memorable, and use voice to give guidance, feedback or a character's voice.

### Initial ideas

Our early sketches went in many directions: a spoon that doubles as a pen with a drawable plate, a talking spoon with proximity and motion sensors, a plate with sub-plates that play songs, a projector that shows content over the plate, and a robot that only charges a phone during mealtime. We chose idea six to take forward. It was a plate that weighs the food to show progress through the meal along a storyline, and unlocks a reward when the meal is finished.

![Notebook of initial idea sketches](/work/bite-buddy/initial-ideas.webp "Initial ideas")

### Concepts

We developed four concepts from that idea and wrote down each one's trade-offs.

- A device that attaches to different plates. Plates and bowls weigh different amounts, so it would have to account for that.
- A weighing base with a screen and buttons. Using different plates could clash visually with the device, and the buttons could distract the child.
- A plate divided into sections that map to the screen, splitting food into small portions. Runny food would be a problem, and setting it up takes time.
- A plate whose screen shows progress as the weight drops, with game templates and characters customised from a companion app and three detachable plates.

The last concept became the selected one.

![Sketch of the selected concept with screen and detachable plate](/work/bite-buddy/selected-concept-sketch.webp "Selected concept: a curved screen around a detachable plate")

{% callout %}
The plate tracks mealtime progress as the food's weight goes down, and the game on the screen moves forward with every bite.
{% /callout %}

### Interactions

Before designing screens, we mapped how someone uses the device. You switch it on with a toggle, pair it through the Bluetooth button, start and pause the game with one button once food is placed, press and hold the same button to reset, and press a release button to unlock and change the plate.

![User interaction map for the device](/work/bite-buddy/interaction-map.webp "Interaction map: pairing, play and pause, reset, detaching the plate")

## Solution

### The plate

The final device is a round base with a curved screen around the top of the rim and three detachable plates that click into place. It's made of food-grade silicone so it's water-resistant and safe to use with food. On the side are an on/off toggle, a play/pause/reset button, a Bluetooth button, a charging port and a plate release button, with status lights for power, battery and Bluetooth. Speakers play voice prompts that encourage mindful eating, and the child gets a reward when the meal is finished.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Top view render with the screen and interchangeable plate](/work/bite-buddy/render-top-view.webp)

![Close-up render of the controls and indicators](/work/bite-buddy/render-controls.webp)
{% /gallery %}

### Device screens

A start-up screen invites the child to press start. Then a game template runs along the rim. As the child eats, the character moves forward and collects coins and learning items like letters and numbers. In the example we designed, it collects question marks that reveal the word "CAT". When the meal is finished, the screen shows the coins earned.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Start-up screen on the plate](/work/bite-buddy/device-startup-screen.webp)

![Game screen revealing the word CAT](/work/bite-buddy/device-game-screen.webp)
{% /gallery %}

### The companion app

The app lets parents change games, customise the character and follow meals. We gave it a playful identity built on the logotype, the Blont and Poppins typefaces, and a purple and teal palette. The buddy character illustrations came from Freepik.

![Companion app logotype, fonts, colours and character illustrations](/work/bite-buddy/app-visual-language.webp "Companion app visual language")

After logging in, the home page shows whether the plate is connected and lists meal history. To start a meal, the parent picks a game (locked games can be unlocked with points), then picks a meal type: clean plate, scrappy or messy. That helps the system estimate completion more accurately. During the meal, the app shows progress live, and the parent can pause, stop or press finish if the child doesn't eat everything. At the end, a summary shows coins earned, how long the meal took and how much food was eaten. Coins can be spent on accessories for the buddy.

{% gallery columns=3 %}
![Home page, disconnected and connected with meal history](/work/bite-buddy/app-home.webp)

![Choosing a meal type and tracking progress](/work/bite-buddy/app-mealtime.webp)

![Customising the buddy with accessories](/work/bite-buddy/app-customize.webp)
{% /gallery %}

## Outcome & learnings

We delivered a complete concept: research, a selected product direction with sketches, an interaction map, product renders, device screens and a companion app. It stayed at concept stage. We didn't build a working prototype or test it with children and parents, so there are no results to report.

Designing the object and the app together was the most useful part for me. Every button on the plate had to make sense alongside what the app does, and the interaction map kept the two consistent. There's also a tension I'd want to look at more honestly next time: we're putting a screen on a plate to pull children away from screens. If I took it further, I'd prototype the weighing and test whether a slow, food-paced game holds a 3 to 5 year old's attention without becoming a new distraction. I'd also want to see how parents feel about the meal tracking.
